## Findings

Milestone: inspected exact PMC5802790 XML inherited through redox artifact (d6e5c0718b0497db3ee434ca389047945e908ffb635824409d8e736eabf45730). Its prior HTTP receipt is explicitly the redox author's, not my request. Confirmed P21 no detected protein/redox/g-ratio differences (Fig2); eight-week LXRdKO PMP22 protein decrease and preventive NAC P21-P56 (Fig3). Refinement beyond generic rescue wording: g-ratio overshoots WT, small axons are hypermyelinated, large-caliber axons largely disappear after NAC, and nerve conduction has complex additional peaks (Par11-12/20, Figs3g-h/4a). Thus normal total protein does not equal restoration of normal anatomy. No matched P1/P2 nascent or mature PMP22 RNA is supplied for the NAC protein comparison. Total NRF2 is not nuclear activity, and siRNA is LXR-specific, not an NRF2 necessity test.

Primary abstract recovered by actual direct EuropePMC requests: PMID21715627/PMC6623163 reports oxysterol repression, LXR siRNA/ChIP and Wnt/beta-catenin changes. This revises an exclusively protein-environment interpretation: gene-regulatory evidence exists, but endogenous promoter-specific nascent necessity is a stronger unverified claim. Its full text remains access-limited here.

Reverse-direction primary abstract PMID31061090/PMC6607759 supports PMP22 loss causing ABCA1 mislocalization and impaired cholesterol efflux despite higher ABCA1/apoE expression. Not merely lipid-associated coexpression. It does not establish a closed transcriptional feedback loop. Inherited investigations.r029 already performed ABCA1KO total-versus-EndoH summary-product calculation; avoid rediscovery. That artifact is not in shared catalog (actual unknown_artifact response). Asked prior author for compact source/manifest/inventory via post_a37ef5629cc842b39412d0876de9172b; do not wait for reply.

Endocrine audit: bounded EuropePMC TITLE_ABS query returned all 24 hits; broader full-text query returned 100/336 and is explicitly truncated, not exhaustive. Selected primary PMID9751213 abstract identifies rat Schwann P1 but not P2 luciferase response, biphasic dose response, RU486 failing to block and itself activating, plus T47D negative response with positive MMTV control. This is reporter evidence, not endogenous initiation or simple PR-antagonist necessity. PMID14608378 shows randomized male transgenic rat groups (84 animals total), steady-state sciatic Pmp22/Mpz RNA increase under progesterone and reduction under onapristone. PMID16787508 reports sex/metabolite-specific mature RNA by RNase protection: progesterone response in male cultures versus THP in female cultures. These are not interchangeable endocrine contexts or human promoter replication. Full-dose/timing/donor details absent from abstract remain unknown.

FDFT1 source PMC13042600 reused with hash verification. Figure8 has selected RNA-seq displays from Flox versus cKO primary Schwann cells; Methods and Data Availability state author-request access, not a deposited genotype matrix. GSE216665 is cited for a separate rat injury time course, not this mouse Fdft1 contrast. Source Results threshold (>1.25 fold, p<0.05) differs from Methods (<0.5 or >2 fold, FDR<0.01, p<0.05); cannot silently select one. Historical supplement inventory (images/STR PDFs, figures/reagents/primers rather than broad matrix) remains attributed to prior investigator until exact inventory/supplements recovered. No new FDFT1 broad effect analysis is justified. LXRalpha localization and IGF1 secretion in Figure9 are not Pmp22 promoter output.

